  'salicylic acid' => 'A beta-hydroxy acid used topically to loosen keratin and treat acne, psoriasis, warts, and calluses; extensive exposure can cause salicylate toxicity.',
  'salt gland' => 'A specialized gland in some marine animals and birds that excretes concentrated salt to maintain fluid and electrolyte balance; humans do not possess a comparable salt gland.',
  'sample fixation' => 'Preservation of a tissue or cell sample with a chemical or physical method, such as formalin fixation, to prevent decay and maintain microscopic structure for processing and staining.',
  'sanitation' => 'Public-health measures that safely manage human waste, water, food, hygiene, and environmental contamination to prevent infection and disease transmission.',
  'sarcocystosis' => 'Parasitic infection by Sarcocystis species, acquired from undercooked meat or contaminated food and sometimes causing muscle pain, fever, diarrhea, or no symptoms.',
  'sarcosinemia' => 'An uncommon metabolic finding with elevated sarcosine in blood or urine, usually due to altered glycine metabolism and often without major clinical symptoms.',
  'satiation' => 'The feeling of fullness that terminates a meal, produced by gastrointestinal stretch, nutrients, hormones, and brain signals regulating food intake.',
  'satisfaction' => 'A person’s evaluation of how well an experience, service, treatment, or outcome met expectations; in healthcare it is one quality measure but does not replace clinical outcomes or safety.',
  'scab' => 'A dry protective crust of clotted blood, serum, pus, and dead tissue over a healing skin injury or erosion.',
  'scan gallium' => 'A nuclear-medicine scan using radiolabeled gallium to identify areas of inflammation, infection, or some tumors; uptake is interpreted with anatomy and clinical context.',
  'scanning' => 'Systematic examination of a body region or specimen using imaging, microscopy, or another detection method to locate, characterize, or monitor abnormalities.',
  'scanning electron microscopy' => 'Microscopy that scans a focused electron beam across a specimen surface to produce a high-resolution image of its three-dimensional topography and composition.',
  'scaphoid bone' => 'The largest proximal-row carpal bone on the thumb side of the wrist; its waist is vulnerable to fracture and avascular necrosis because of its blood supply.',
  'scar' => 'Fibrous tissue that replaces normal skin or other tissue after injury, surgery, inflammation, or burns and may be flat, raised, contracted, painful, or itchy.',
  'scavenger receptor' => 'A cell-surface or intracellular receptor that binds and removes modified lipids, debris, microbes, or dying cells and contributes to innate immunity and atherosclerosis.',
  'scheduled notification' => 'A planned alert or message delivered at a specified time to remind a person about an appointment, medicine, monitoring task, or health-related action.',
  'Schiller test' => 'A cervical examination using iodine solution; glycogen-rich normal squamous epithelium stains dark, while iodine-negative areas require clinical correlation and sometimes biopsy.',
  'schistocyte count' => 'Measurement of fragmented red blood cells on a blood smear, supporting evaluation of microangiopathic hemolysis, mechanical valve damage, disseminated intravascular coagulation, or severe burns.',
  'screening procedure' => 'A test or examination offered to people without symptoms to detect disease or risk early enough for effective prevention or treatment; eligibility and intervals depend on evidence and risk.',
  'screening study' => 'A planned investigation of an asymptomatic population to identify early disease, precursor lesions, or risk factors using a validated test and follow-up pathway.',
  'secondary amenorrhea' => 'Absence of menstruation for at least three months in someone with previously regular cycles or six months with previously irregular cycles, caused by pregnancy, endocrine, ovarian, uterine, or systemic factors.',
  'secondary hypothyroidism' => 'Low thyroid hormone production caused by inadequate pituitary TSH stimulation, usually from pituitary or hypothalamic disease; TSH may be low, normal, or inappropriately mildly high.',
  'secondary metabolism' => 'Biochemical pathways that produce specialized compounds not required for basic growth but important for defense, signaling, pigmentation, or interactions with the environment.',
  'silent mutation' => 'A DNA sequence change that does not alter the encoded amino acid, usually because of genetic-code redundancy, although it can sometimes affect splicing, RNA stability, or translation.',
  'sinus histiocytosis' => 'Expansion of lymph-node sinuses by histiocytes, often a reactive response to drainage of inflammation, infection, tumor, or tissue breakdown; interpretation depends on associated findings.',
  'site-directed mutagenesis' => 'A laboratory method that introduces a planned nucleotide change at a selected DNA site to study gene function, protein structure, regulation, or disease variants.',
  'size perception' => 'The sensory judgment of an object’s dimensions, influenced by visual context, distance, depth cues, touch, and brain processing; neurologic or visual disease can distort it.',
  'skeletal muscle tissue' => 'Voluntary striated muscle attached to bones or other structures, composed of contractile fibers that generate movement, posture, heat, and joint stability.',
  'skin care' => 'Measures that protect and maintain the skin, including gentle cleansing, moisturization, sun protection, wound care, avoidance of irritants, and treatment of identified disease.',
  'skin erosion' => 'A shallow loss of the epidermis that leaves a moist or crusted surface but does not extend deeply into the dermis, often caused by friction, scratching, blister rupture, or inflammation.',
  'skin examination' => 'Systematic inspection and palpation of the skin, hair, nails, and mucous membranes to assess color, lesions, texture, distribution, vascular signs, infection, and cancer warning features.',
  'skin induration' => 'Abnormal hardening or firmness of skin or underlying tissue caused by fibrosis, inflammation, edema, infiltrative disease, infection, or tumor.',
  'skin mastocytoma' => 'A localized collection of mast cells in the skin, usually a yellow-brown or reddish plaque or nodule in a child that may swell, itch, or redden when rubbed.',
  'skin necrosis' => 'Death of skin tissue from ischemia, infection, pressure, toxins, trauma, vasculitis, or severe inflammation, producing discoloration, blisters, ulcers, or black eschar.',
  'spinal artery' => 'An artery supplying the spinal cord and its coverings, including the anterior spinal artery and paired posterior spinal arteries; blockage can cause characteristic neurologic deficits.',
  'spinal ganglion' => 'A cluster of sensory neuron cell bodies, especially a dorsal-root ganglion, carrying signals from skin, muscles, and joints toward the spinal cord.',
  'spiral colon' => 'A spiral arrangement of the proximal colon described in some animals, especially ruminants, that increases transit length and absorptive surface; it is not a normal human colonic segment.',
  'splanchnic nerve' => 'A visceral autonomic nerve carrying sympathetic or parasympathetic fibers from the thorax or abdomen to prevertebral ganglia and internal organs.',
  'splenic abscess' => 'A localized collection of pus in the spleen, usually from bloodstream infection, trauma, infarction, or spread from nearby disease and potentially causing fever, left-upper-quadrant pain, and sepsis.',
  'split brain' => 'A state after surgical or pathological disruption of the corpus callosum in which information transfer between cerebral hemispheres is reduced, producing characteristic disconnection findings.',
  'spondylarthritis' => 'A family of inflammatory rheumatic diseases affecting the spine, sacroiliac joints, entheses, peripheral joints, skin, eyes, or bowel, including ankylosing spondylitis and psoriatic arthritis.',
  'spontaneous abortion' => 'Pregnancy loss before fetal viability that occurs without intentional intervention, commonly caused by chromosomal abnormalities but also by uterine, endocrine, immune, infectious, or other factors.',
  'spontaneous perforation' => 'Unexpected rupture of a hollow organ without a clear external injury, allowing contents to escape and potentially causing peritonitis, mediastinitis, sepsis, or hemorrhage.',
  'sscp analysis' => 'Single-strand conformation polymorphism analysis, a laboratory method that detects some DNA sequence variants by differences in the migration of single-stranded fragments through a gel.',
  'sympathetic ganglion' => 'A cluster of sympathetic autonomic neuron cell bodies, located in paravertebral or prevertebral chains, that relays signals controlling vessels, sweat glands, viscera, and other targets.',
  'synaptic membrane' => 'The specialized membrane at a neuronal junction, including the neurotransmitter-releasing presynaptic membrane and receptor-containing postsynaptic membrane.',
  'synarthrosis' => 'A joint with little or no movement, such as a skull suture or tooth anchored in its socket, in which bones are joined by fibrous tissue or cartilage.',
  'syngeneic' => 'Genetically identical or nearly identical within the same species, as in tissues or cells exchanged between identical twins or inbred laboratory animals.',
  'synovial membrane' => 'The inner lining of a synovial joint capsule that produces synovial fluid and contains cells involved in lubrication, nutrition, inflammation, and joint disease.',
  'systemic scleroderma' => 'Systemic sclerosis, an autoimmune connective-tissue disease causing vascular dysfunction and fibrosis of skin and internal organs, commonly affecting the esophagus, lungs, kidneys, and heart.',
  'Swelling' => 'Abnormal enlargement caused by fluid accumulation, inflammation, bleeding, infection, tumor, or tissue injury; location, onset, pain, and associated symptoms identify the likely cause.',
  'Scar revision' => 'A procedure that improves the appearance, contour, flexibility, or symptoms of a scar through excision, rearrangement, grafting, laser, injection, or other selected techniques.',
  'Screen time and children' => 'Time children spend using televisions, computers, phones, tablets, or game devices; excessive or poorly timed use can affect sleep, activity, learning, mood, and family routines.',
  'Scrotal swelling' => 'Enlargement of the scrotum caused by fluid, inflammation, infection, hernia, varicocele, hydrocele, tumor, trauma, or testicular torsion; sudden pain is an emergency.',
  'Self catheterization - female' => 'Intermittent insertion and removal of a catheter through the female urethra to empty the bladder when normal urination is inadequate, reducing retention and protecting kidney function.',
  'Self catheterization - male' => 'Intermittent insertion and removal of a catheter through the male urethra to drain the bladder when neurologic, obstructive, or other conditions prevent adequate emptying.',
  'Separation anxiety in children' => 'Developmentally excessive distress when separated from an attachment figure, with worry, refusal, physical symptoms, nightmares, or school avoidance that impairs daily life.',
  'Skin flaps and grafts - self-care' => 'Care after reconstructive skin surgery that protects the flap or graft, keeps dressings clean, avoids pressure and smoking, monitors color and temperature, and reports infection or loss of blood supply.',
  'Small for gestational age (SGA)' => 'Birth weight or size below the expected range for gestational age, caused by constitutional small size, placental insufficiency, infection, genetic disease, or maternal factors.',
  'Smell - impaired' => 'Reduced or absent ability to detect odors, called hyposmia or anosmia, caused by nasal obstruction, viral injury, head trauma, neurodegeneration, medications, or other disease.',
  'Smoking cessation medications' => 'Medicines that reduce nicotine withdrawal and cravings, including nicotine replacement, bupropion, and varenicline, used with behavioral support and individualized for health risks and preferences.',
  'Snacking when you have diabetes' => 'Planning snacks for diabetes by considering hunger, glucose pattern, activity, carbohydrate amount, medicine timing, and hypoglycemia risk rather than relying on unrestricted grazing.',
  'Snoring - adults' => 'Noisy breathing during sleep caused by vibration of relaxed tissues in the nose, mouth, or throat; loud habitual snoring with witnessed pauses or daytime sleepiness may indicate obstructive sleep apnea.',
  'Spasmus nutans' => 'An uncommon early-childhood syndrome of rapid shimmering eye movements, head nodding, and torticollis that usually resolves but requires evaluation to exclude neurologic or ocular disease.',
  'Speech impairment in adults' => 'Difficulty producing or understanding speech caused by aphasia, dysarthria, apraxia, hearing loss, neurologic disease, structural problems, or cognitive disorder; sudden onset requires stroke assessment.',
  'Spinal cord stimulation' => 'An implanted treatment that delivers electrical impulses to the epidural space to alter pain signaling, used for selected chronic neuropathic or ischemic pain after careful assessment.',
  'Sternal exploration or closure' => 'Surgical reopening or repair of the breastbone after median sternotomy, performed to treat infection, bleeding, instability, dehiscence, or other postoperative chest complications.',
  'Stools – floating' => 'Feces that remain on the water surface, often from trapped gas or diet but sometimes from excess fat in malabsorption; persistent greasy, pale, foul-smelling stool needs evaluation.',
  'Stools - foul smelling' => 'Strongly odorous feces that may result from diet, infection, bleeding, malabsorption, altered gut bacteria, or rapid transit; persistent change with weight loss or diarrhea warrants review.',
  'Stools - pale or clay-colored' => 'Light-colored stool caused by reduced bile pigment reaching the intestine, potentially indicating bile-duct obstruction, gallbladder disease, liver disease, or certain transient conditions.',
  'Stretch marks' => 'Linear bands of atrophic skin, also called striae, caused by stretching and hormonal or corticosteroid effects; they commonly occur during pregnancy, growth, weight change, or steroid exposure.',
  'Stuttering' => 'A speech-fluency disorder involving repeated sounds or words, prolongations, blocks, and associated tension or avoidance; onset, developmental course, and impact guide therapy.',
  'Subareolar abscess' => 'A pus-filled infection beneath the areola, often associated with periductal inflammation and smoking, causing a painful lump, redness, drainage, or recurrent fistula.',
  'Subdural effusion' => 'Accumulation of usually clear fluid in the subdural space, often after trauma, surgery, infection, or cerebrospinal-fluid disturbance and sometimes causing headache or pressure effects.',
  'Suicide and suicidal behavior' => 'Thoughts, plans, attempts, or acts intended to cause one’s own death; any current intent, plan, access to means, or inability to stay safe requires immediate emergency support.',
  'Swan-Ganz - right heart catheterization' => 'Insertion of a pulmonary-artery catheter through the right heart to measure central venous, pulmonary-artery, and wedge pressures and cardiac output in selected critically ill patients.',
  'Sydenham chorea' => 'A delayed neurologic complication of group A streptococcal infection causing involuntary jerky movements, hypotonia, emotional lability, and coordination problems, usually in children.',
  'S1-S5 (sacral vertebrae)' => 'The five sacral vertebrae that fuse to form the sacrum, connect the spine to the pelvis, and provide foramina for sacral nerve roots.',
  'Section' => 'A defined part or subdivision of an organ, document, study, or procedure; in anatomy and imaging, the term identifies a specific slice or region examined.',
  'Salabrasion' => 'A dermabrasion method using salt or an abrasive salt preparation to remove superficial skin layers; it carries risks of pain, infection, pigment change, and scarring.',
  'Scabicide' => 'A medicine used to kill Sarcoptes scabiei mites and treat scabies, usually combined with treatment of close contacts, laundering of fabrics, and management of persistent itch.',
  'Scarification' => 'Intentional scratching, cutting, or abrasion of skin, historically used in some inoculation or cultural practices and medically used in limited procedures; it can cause infection and scarring.',
  'Sclerosis' => 'Abnormal hardening or scarring of tissue, often from excess connective tissue, chronic inflammation, degeneration, or neurologic plaques; the affected organ determines the specific disease.',
  'Section, Cesarean' => 'Cesarean section, an operation delivering a baby through incisions in the abdomen and uterus when vaginal birth is unsafe, impossible, or not advisable.',
  'Sedimentation rate' => 'The erythrocyte sedimentation rate, a nonspecific blood test measuring how quickly red cells settle in a tube and sometimes supporting assessment of inflammation when interpreted with other findings.',
  'Seizure disorders' => 'Conditions characterized by recurrent unprovoked seizures or an enduring predisposition to them, classified by seizure type and cause and treated according to epilepsy syndrome and risk.',
  'Senile' => 'An outdated and stigmatizing adjective historically used to attribute changes to old age; modern descriptions should specify the actual condition, such as dementia, frailty, or age-related change.',
  'Sensation' => 'Conscious awareness of stimuli such as touch, pain, temperature, vibration, position, sound, light, taste, or smell produced by sensory receptors and nervous-system processing.',
  'Septic' => 'Relating to infection or contamination by pathogenic microorganisms; in “septic shock” it refers to life-threatening circulatory and metabolic dysfunction caused by an abnormal response to infection.',
  'Septum, heart' => 'The wall separating the right and left chambers of the heart, consisting of atrial and ventricular portions and preventing normal mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood.',
  'Sequela' => 'A lasting consequence or condition that remains after an earlier disease, injury, infection, or treatment has ended, such as paralysis after stroke or scarring after infection.',
  'Serositis' => 'Inflammation of a serous membrane, such as the pleura, pericardium, or peritoneum, causing pain with breathing or movement and sometimes an inflammatory effusion.',
  'Serration' => 'A row of small tooth-like projections or notches along an edge, used to describe anatomy, cells, instruments, or imaging appearances.',
  'Ship fever' => 'An historical term for epidemic typhus, a louse-borne infection caused by Rickettsia prowazekii and associated with crowding, fever, rash, and severe systemic illness.',
  'Shock, secondary' => 'Circulatory failure occurring as a consequence of another condition, such as infection, trauma, bleeding, heart failure, allergy, or endocrine crisis, with inadequate tissue perfusion.',
  'Short arm of a chromosome' => 'The p arm, or shorter arm, of a chromosome, named from the French petit; cytogenetic bands on this arm locate genes and chromosomal abnormalities.',
  'Situational syncope' => 'A reflex faint triggered by a specific situation such as coughing, urination, defecation, swallowing, laughter, or prolonged standing, caused by transient reduced cerebral perfusion.',
  'Skeleton, bones of the' => 'The body’s framework of bones and cartilage, providing support, protection, leverage for movement, mineral storage, and marrow-based blood-cell production.',
  'Smell' => 'The sense of detecting and identifying airborne chemical molecules through receptors in the nasal olfactory epithelium and processing in the olfactory brain pathways.',
  'Sociophobia' => 'An older term for social anxiety disorder, involving excessive fear of scrutiny, embarrassment, humiliation, or negative evaluation in social or performance situations.',
  'Somnolence' => 'Abnormal sleepiness or a tendency to fall asleep during wakeful periods, caused by insufficient sleep, medicines, sleep disorders, metabolic disease, or neurologic illness.',
  'Sore' => 'A painful or tender area, lesion, or wound affecting skin, mucosa, muscle, or another tissue; the appearance, duration, drainage, and associated symptoms identify its cause.',
  'Spastic colitis' => 'An outdated term often used for irritable bowel syndrome, a disorder of recurrent abdominal pain with altered bowel habits without a structural explanation.',
  'Speech-language  pathologist' => 'A clinician who evaluates and treats disorders of speech, language, voice, communication, cognition, and swallowing across childhood, adulthood, and older age.',
  'Spermatogenesis' => 'The process in the seminiferous tubules by which spermatogonial stem cells develop through meiosis and maturation into spermatozoa.',
  'Spots in front of the eyes' => 'Floaters or visual spots caused by shadows from vitreous opacities, inflammation, bleeding, retinal tears, migraine, or other eye or neurologic conditions; sudden flashes or a curtain-like loss are urgent.',
  'Squamous cells' => 'Flat epithelial cells with scale-like appearance that line surfaces such as skin, mouth, cervix, and esophagus; abnormal numbers or forms can reflect irritation, infection, dysplasia, or cancer.',
  'Sternutation' => 'Sneezing, a reflex forceful expulsion of air through the nose and mouth triggered by irritation of nasal or upper-airway sensory receptors.',
  'Stomach cramps' => 'Intermittent gripping or spasmodic upper-abdominal pain caused by gas, infection, indigestion, ulcer disease, gallbladder or pancreatic disease, obstruction, or other causes.',
  'Stomach fat (belly fat, abdominal fat)' => 'Adipose tissue stored beneath the skin or around abdominal organs; excess visceral fat is metabolically active and associated with insulin resistance, fatty liver, and cardiovascular risk.',
  'Stool' => 'Fecal material expelled from the bowel, composed of water, undigested residue, bacteria, cells, mucus, and bile pigments; color, form, frequency, and blood can provide clinical clues.',
  'Study population' => 'The defined group of people included in a research study, selected according to eligibility criteria and used to estimate effects, associations, or outcomes.',
  'Subaortic stenosis' => 'Narrowing of the left-ventricular outflow tract just below the aortic valve, often caused by a fibrous ridge or tunnel and producing a pressure gradient, murmur, and possible aortic-valve injury.',
  'Subdural' => 'Located beneath the dura mater, the tough outer membrane surrounding the brain and spinal cord; subdural bleeding or fluid can compress neural tissue.',
  'Subway diet' => 'A commercial weight-loss approach emphasizing low-calorie meals from a restaurant chain; healthfulness depends on total nutrients, sodium, portion size, sustainability, and individual medical needs.',
  'Sundowning' => 'Late-day or evening worsening of confusion, agitation, wandering, or behavioral symptoms in some people with dementia or delirium, influenced by fatigue, light, routines, and unmet needs.',
  'Sweating, gustatory' => 'Sweating triggered by eating or smelling food, usually over the cheek or temple after parotid-nerve injury or surgery and known as Frey syndrome.',
  'Syncope, coughing' => 'A brief faint caused by coughing, which raises chest pressure, reduces venous return and cardiac output, and transiently lowers cerebral blood flow.',
  'Swallowing sunscreen' => 'Ingestion of sunscreen, usually causing mild gastrointestinal upset but potentially causing choking or aspiration depending on amount and product; breathing difficulty or persistent vomiting requires urgent help.'
}.freeze

text = File.read(FILE)
changed = 0
text = text.gsub(/^\\medterm\{([^}]*)\} ([^\n]*)/) do |line|
  term = Regexp.last_match(1)
  old = Regexp.last_match(2)
  next line unless old.match?(/is a medical term used to describe|is a disease, symptom, syndrome|is an anatomical or histologic|is a biological, genetic|is a healthcare or clinical-care|is a diagnostic or laboratory/i)
  replacement = SPECIAL[term]
  next line unless replacement
  changed += 1
  "\\medterm{#{term}} #{replacement}"
end
File.write(FILE, text)
puts "updated=#{changed}"
